{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 30, :comments ["<b>Of the seed of the land.</b> To include garlic, onions, and cress. From here the Rav Rabbi Yehudah HeHasid would resolve what people ask regarding what we say in the Gemara: one who separates his challah while it is flour — it is not challah, and it is theft in the hand of the priest, because it is written ״עריסותיכם״. And they ask: if so, why do we not say likewise that one who separates his tithes while they are stalks — they are not tithe, because it is written ״דגנך״? But this is resolved, because regarding tithe it is written ״וכל מעשר הארץ מזרע הארץ״, which the Sifra includes: all edible things that are sown in the threshing-floor, such as garlic, onions, and cress. And some explain that since the verse ״מעשר מן המעשר״ is needed to exempt the Levite from terumah gedolah when the Israelite set aside his tithes in stalks, as is concluded in tractate Betzah: 'I said to you ״מעשר מן המעשר״ and not terumah gedolah' — and terumat ma'aser — this implies that the name of tithe does take effect upon it properly even when he set it aside in stalks. However, to the question that if so, even if he set it aside in the pile [kari] it is also a difficulty — we can say that the verse ״מעשר מן המעשר״ was not needed to exempt the Levite from terumah gedolah except where he set it aside in the pile, for then the name of tithe takes effect; but when he set it aside in stalks, where the name of tithe does not take effect upon it because it is written ״מעשר דגנך״, the verse is not needed — and the difficulty returns to its place: why is it not theft in the hand of the Levite? But one can say: according to the conclusion, it works out, for the answer given is 'this one was processed into grain and this one was not processed into grain.' If so, to exempt him from terumah gedolah when he set it aside in the pile is impossible, and if so the verse ״מעשר מן המעשר״ comes to allow setting it aside in stalks — implying that when it is in stalks the name of tithe does take effect upon it. And the one who understands will understand."], :book :leviticus, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {0 "The comment cites a passage from tractates Challah/Betzah about separating challah from flour vs. tithes from stalks; the crops listed (שום ושחלים וגרגיר) are identified conventionally as garlic, onions, and cress. The logic is intricate and preserves the Talmudic terminology throughout without smoothing."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:08:50.597025779Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}